{"reference":"2 Thessalonians 3","book":"2 Thessalonians","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter opens by joining the senders’ need for prayer to their hope that the Lord’s message will spread and receive honor. It closes with a wish for peace and the Lord’s presence for the whole community, framing the intervening correction of disorderly conduct within concern for the community’s well-being.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 3:1-2","2 Thessalonians 3:16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_003_2230409e5f4e2bf9d9b96a8c","reader_path_2_thessalonians_003_6c1df94fa7095c0b4bf6a8c9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"A Key Distinction","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The condition in verse 10 concerns someone unwilling to work: it addresses refusal, not inability. That distinction is important for reading the command precisely rather than treating it as a statement about every person who lacks work.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 3:10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_003_bd90d6f90a07fefee3898b0d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Work, Correction, and Community","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The reported problem is not simply that some people lack employment. The letter describes some as doing no work while meddling in others’ affairs, and directs those people to work quietly and provide their own bread. Its corrective action is also bounded: the community is to take note of persistent disobedience and limit association, yet still admonish the person as a sibling rather than regard them as an enemy.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 3:10","2 Thessalonians 3:12","2 Thessalonians 3:14-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_003_fb8e376949619325342089af"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The senders present their own strenuous labor and refusal of free bread as an example, while explicitly saying they had a right they chose not to use. The final greeting also presents handwriting as a sign associated with Paul’s letters. Modern scholarship remains divided over the letter’s authorship and over how that closing claim should be weighed historically.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 3:11-12","2 Thessalonians 3:14-15","2 Thessalonians 3:17","2 Thessalonians 3:7-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_003_d3a9536241f1c7bed73a814c"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why This Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The command about work should be read alongside both the description of disruptive behavior and the instruction not to grow weary in doing good. The senders’ choice not to claim support is presented as an example, not as the abolition of support rights. Together, these features keep the passage from being reduced to a judgment about low income or unemployment and prevent discipline from displacing mutual aid, generosity, or patient care.","verse_refs":["2 Thessalonians 3:11","2 Thessalonians 3:13","2 Thessalonians 3:7-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_thessalonians_003_f2b03565222242aa94b39e17"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}